            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Personal Life and Inspirations</strong><br>\nTennyson drew inspiration from the natural landscape and often infused his poetry with a love for the natural world. His personal experiences with loss and melancholy imbued his work with depth and empathy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Peerage</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Tennyson accepted a peerage, becoming the first English writer raised to the nobility for his literary contributions. He continued to write poetry that resonated with a wide audience.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nAlfred Tennyson died on October 6, 1892, at the age of 83. His legacy endures in the timeless quality of his poetry and his contribution to the canon of English literature.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
